It's inconvenient for the bulk-read operation to kill any overlarge read when the actual read would've been fine — especially since `BufferedReader` and `BufferedInputStream` also uses 8 KiB buffers, which would consume the default limit in one go. On the other hand, checking the bytes consumed after the fact is also doesn't seem right.

We could split the difference and let the bulk-read operation read up to the limit, and only throw if reads are done at the limit and beyond.

